{
    "title": "Waste Prevention, Production Subject to Royalties, and Resource Conservation; Extension of Phase-In Requirements",
    "type": "Rule",
    "date": "December 15, 2025",
    "url": "https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/FR-2025-12-15/pdf/2025-22820.pdf",
    "summary": "The Interior Department just gave oil and gas operators more time to meet two methane control requirements on federal and tribal lands, without changing the rules themselves. It also restored a definition that clarifies which Arctic offshore areas are covered by certain regulations.\n\nHere\u2019s what changed. The Bureau of Land Management is extending the deadline to install meters and take gas samples at smaller, high\u2011pressure flares, and to submit statewide leak detection and repair plans. Instead of December 10, 2025, the new deadline is December 10, 2026. The stricter rules for larger flares are already in place and stay that way. The agency says it\u2019s working on another rule that could affect timelines, and this extra year is meant to avoid forcing companies to comply twice in quick succession. Separately, the definition of the Arctic Outer Continental Shelf\u2014covering the Beaufort and Chukchi seas\u2014has been reinstated in federal regulations to keep that scope clear.\n\nWho benefits? Oil and gas operators, especially small and mid\u2011sized producers, get breathing room to plan, budget, and line up equipment and contractors. Companies that operate many leases across a state also get more time to pull together a single leak detection plan, which can be complex. Investors and lenders may welcome the reduced near\u2011term costs and clearer timing. Regulators benefit from avoiding rapid changes if another proposal is coming soon, and the public still gets the same standards\u2014just on a later schedule.\n\nWho might see downsides? Communities concerned about air quality and climate impacts may worry that delaying leak checks and measurement for smaller flares prolongs methane and other emissions. Taxpayers, tribes, and states that rely on accurate measurements to calculate royalties could see delayed improvements in data quality. Companies that already spent money to comply on the original timeline may feel they\u2019re at a disadvantage compared to peers who waited. And because parts of the underlying 2024 rule are on hold in some states due to ongoing lawsuits, the policy landscape remains uneven.\n\nThe direct final rule takes effect February 13, 2026, unless significant public comments prompt a change. For now, the message is simple: same standards, more time, and a clearer map for where Arctic offshore rules apply.",
